        <p>The last column holds the pattern. At 20 hours a week you keep $53.51 an hour; at 40 hours you keep $49.16. That $4.35 gap on the same $64.00 rate comes from the bracket: only $60.00 of a 20-hour year’s $50,460.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket, while a 25-hour year’s $67,100.00 of taxable income runs $16,700.00 into it, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up in any real size. Three rows matter more than the headline. The 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own at $116,480.00, and its $90,773.68 of take-home already beats a $110,000 salary by $4,558.68, before overtime or a raise. The 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $124,800.00 gross, and it beats a $120,000 salary by $4,800.00 gross and $3,316.80 of take-home; at $63 the same week beat the $120,000 salary by only $1,983.97, so the lead has nearly doubled in one rung. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $8,320.00 gross and $5,686.72 of take-home. And the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $33,280.00 gross and $23,186.08 of take-home, $1,932.17 a month, the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Two more results are worth knowing. A 25-hour week at $64 beats a $75,000 salary’s take-home by $5,768.70, so even a part-time schedule at this rate out-earns a solid full-time salary. Part-time keeps the bigger share, 83.6% of gross at 20 hours against 76.8% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $64 beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $9,286.20. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
