      <section class="article-section key-takeaways" id="key-takeaways">
        <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Action Plan</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>$64 an hour is $133,120 a year full-time: $2,560 a week, $5,120 biweekly, $11,093.33 a month on average, before taxes.</li>
          <li>Federal-only take-home is $102,253.52 for a single filer. That is $8,521.13 a month, $3,932.83 biweekly, and $49.16 kept per hour, at a 24% marginal rate.</li>
          <li>A four-day week at $64 still beats six figures: 32 hours a week keeps $83,749.94, which beats a $100,000 salary’s take-home by $4,569.94. Each Friday off costs $355.84 of take-home.</li>
          <li>A part-time hour keeps more than a full-time hour: $53.51 at 20 hours a week against $49.16 at 40. And a 25-hour week at $64 already beats a $75,000 salary’s take-home by $5,768.70. A 35-hour week alone crosses $100,000 gross.</li>
          <li>$11,320 of your income reaches the 24% bracket, costing $2,716.80. That is why your effective federal rate is 15.5%, not 24%.</li>
          <li>A $1 raise from here keeps only $1,421.68, down from the flat $1,463.28 every raise kept up to $58.56 an hour, and a 5% raise to $67.20 pushes take-home to $106,802.90, past a $135,000 salary, with no extra hours, yet it still trails a $140,000 salary by $153.10.</li>
          <li>Five overtime hours a week add $24,960 a year ($158,080 total; $119,313.68 take-home). At $64 they push $36,280 of income into the 24% bracket, costing $8,707.20, and every overtime hour keeps $65.62. Two overtime hours a week already beat a $140,000 salary.</li>
          <li>$64 beats a $130,000 salary on take-home alone, $2,132.52 ahead, and trails a $135,000 salary by only $1,880 of gross. The $125,000 salary’s 3% match now loses by $1,800.02, and only 20 overtime hours a year close the $135,000 line.</li>
          <li>One unpaid week costs $1,749.76 of take-home, more than a $1 raise is worth, and it drops you $328.08 below a full-time $63-an-hour year. Even thirteen unpaid weeks trail a $100,000 salary by only $112.56. Compare on paid weeks and real hours, not the headline rate.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="source-note">Sources and method: Federal figures use the 2026 tax brackets, the $16,100 standard deduction for single filers, and the 7.65% FICA rate (6.2% Social Security up to the $184,500 wage base, 1.45% Medicare), from IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32. The 2,080-hour year is the standard 40-hour-week convention, and monthly pay is gross divided by 12, not by 4. Three independent checks back the headline: takehomepay.online puts federal tax at $21,487.30, $804.50 too high, and its headline is a California figure; pineapplemoney lands $2,746.52 low at $99,507 on a federal figure $2,578.80 too low; applyquick.ai uses 2025 brackets and lands $332.52 low at about $101,921. Wage benchmarks are Bureau of Labor Statistics OEWS May 2025 (median $24.51 an hour, 75th percentile $38.71) and BLS median weekly earnings of $1,251 in Q2 2026.</p>      </section>
